  "content": {
    "id": "p11-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Find the values of $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi]$ for which:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p11-q3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\sin3\\theta+\\sin\\theta=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pi,\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},2\\pi$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\sin3\\theta+\\sin\\theta&=2\\sin2\\theta\\cos\\theta\\\\2\\sin2\\theta\\cos\\theta&=0\\end{align*}$$\nBy the null-factor law, $\\sin2\\theta=0$ or $\\cos\\theta=0$.\nFor $2\\theta\\in[0,4\\pi]$, $\\sin2\\theta=0$ gives $2\\theta=0,\\pi,2\\pi,3\\pi,4\\pi$, so $\\theta=0,\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi,\\frac{3\\pi}{2},2\\pi$.\nThe solutions of $\\cos\\theta=0$ are $\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, already included.\nTherefore, $\\theta=0,\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi,\\frac{3\\pi}{2},2\\pi$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\cos2\\theta-\\cos\\theta=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\theta=0,\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3},2\\pi$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\cos2\\theta-\\cos\\theta&=-2\\sin\\frac{3\\theta}{2}\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\\\-2\\sin\\frac{3\\theta}{2}\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}&=0\\end{align*}$$\nBy the null-factor law, $\\sin\\frac{3\\theta}{2}=0$ or $\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}=0$.\nFor $\\frac{3\\theta}{2}\\in[0,3\\pi]$, the first equation gives $\\frac{3\\theta}{2}=0,\\pi,2\\pi,3\\pi$, so $\\theta=0,\\frac{2\\pi}{3},\\frac{4\\pi}{3},2\\pi$.\nFor $\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\in[0,\\pi]$, the second equation gives $\\theta=0,2\\pi$, already included.\nTherefore, $\\theta=0,\\frac{2\\pi}{3},\\frac{4\\pi}{3},2\\pi$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q3-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$\\sin3\\theta-\\sin2\\theta=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{5},\\dfrac{3\\pi}{5},\\pi,\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5},\\dfrac{9\\pi}{5},2\\pi$.\n\nUse the sine-difference formula and the null-factor law.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\sin3\\theta-\\sin2\\theta&=2\\cos\\frac{5\\theta}{2}\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\\\2\\cos\\frac{5\\theta}{2}\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}&=0\\end{align*}$$\nBy the null-factor law, $\\cos\\frac{5\\theta}{2}=0$ or $\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}=0$.\nFor $\\frac{5\\theta}{2}\\in[0,5\\pi]$, the first equation gives $\\frac{5\\theta}{2}=\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{3\\pi}{2},\\frac{5\\pi}{2},\\frac{7\\pi}{2},\\frac{9\\pi}{2}$, so $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{5},\\frac{3\\pi}{5},\\pi,\\frac{7\\pi}{5},\\frac{9\\pi}{5}$.\nFor $\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\in[0,\\pi]$, the second equation gives $\\theta=0,2\\pi$.\nTherefore, $\\theta=0,\\frac{\\pi}{5},\\frac{3\\pi}{5},\\pi,\\frac{7\\pi}{5},\\frac{9\\pi}{5},2\\pi$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q3-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$\\cos\\theta+\\cos3\\theta=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4},\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\cos\\theta+\\cos3\\theta&=2\\cos2\\theta\\cos\\theta\\\\2\\cos2\\theta\\cos\\theta&=0\\end{align*}$$\nBy the null-factor law, $\\cos2\\theta=0$ or $\\cos\\theta=0$.\nFor $2\\theta\\in[0,4\\pi]$, the first equation gives $2\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{3\\pi}{2},\\frac{5\\pi}{2},\\frac{7\\pi}{2}$, so $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\frac{3\\pi}{4},\\frac{5\\pi}{4},\\frac{7\\pi}{4}$.\nThe second equation gives $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$.\nTherefore, $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{3\\pi}{4},\\frac{5\\pi}{4},\\frac{3\\pi}{2},\\frac{7\\pi}{4}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 50
      }
    ]
  },
